{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Moses returned.</b> The plain sense: why is 'and he sat' stated, and afterwards 'and he told'? Because at first Moses came to return the words of Israel to the Holy One, blessed be He, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Wait, for I wish to speak with you — and He spoke with him the utterance, 'Behold, I come to you.' And afterwards, when the Holy One, blessed be He, finished the utterance, 'Moses told the words of the people to HaShem.' And in tractate Shabbat, chapter אמר רבי עקיבא, it is concluded that this 'Moses sat' and 'Moses told' was the command of the bounding — these are the words of Rabbi Yose ben Yehudah. And Rabbi says: At first he stated its reward — and that is 'Moses sat,' i.e., matters settled to the heart; and at the end he explained its punishment — as it is written 'Moses told,' i.e., matters hard as sinews, which is bitter herbs. And it was the day of Shabbat when the Torah was given, as is proven in the chapter רבי עקיבא. And thus it is said: ״בשובה ונחת תושעון״ — this is the Torah, which was given at first on Shabbat, which is a day of rest; and the latter tablets, which Moses brought down on the Day of Atonement, which is a day of repentance."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:27.896798799Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}